# mgbilly.msg - Billy Creel, Megaton's one-eyed booster (mgbilly.ssl).
# 100-102 look, 110-127 floating remarks, 200+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a weathered man with a patch over one eye and a grin under the other.}
{101}{}{You see Billy Creel. He looks pleased to see you. He looks pleased to see the wall.}
{102}{}{The eyepatch is cut from a boot tongue. The rest of him has been mended the same way: often, cheaply, and with good humor.}

# 110-119: any day.
{110}{}{Finest crater in the wasteland. I've seen a few.}
{111}{}{Walls, water and a sheriff. Name me another town with all three.}
{112}{}{Thirty years of caravans, and this is the only place I ever unpacked.}
{113}{}{You want raiders? Try anywhere else. We're fresh out.}
{114}{}{Maggie! ...No, never mind. She's fine. She's always fine.}
{115}{}{Roof only leaks when it rains. That's twice a year. I call that dry.}
{116}{}{People complain about the bomb. Beats a neighbor who plays the fiddle.}
{117}{}{One eye's plenty. Town's not that big.}
{118}{}{Smell that? Stahl's got the stew on. You can't get that in Klamath. You wouldn't want to.}
{119}{}{I've slept in ditches with worse neighbors than a bomb.}

# 120-122: the bomb is disarmed.
{120}{}{Always said that bomb was nothing to fret about. Nobody fretted harder than me.}
{121}{}{A town with a dead bomb. We're practically respectable.}
{122}{}{Rents'll go up now. You watch.}

# 125-127: Simms is dead.
{125}{}{Somebody ought to pick up that badge. Not me. I only have the one eye.}
{126}{}{He'd have hated the fuss. We'll fuss anyway.}
{127}{}{Still the best town there is. Just quieter about it.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{A new face! Billy Creel. Thirty years guarding other people's cargo over every mile of dirt there is, and I'm here to tell you this is the best town on any of them. Sit down. No chairs. Lean.}
{201}{}{There you are. How's Megaton treating you? Don't say "like a crater." Everybody says that.}
{202}{}{The bomb tamer, in my house! I told Maggie you had steady hands. What I said was "shifty," but I meant steady. Here, I've been saving these for a day worth drinking to.}
{203}{}{You heard about Lucas. Town feels like a wagon on three wheels. We'll roll. We always roll. Slower.}
{204}{}{The bomb tamer! Pull up some wall. Anything you want to know about this town, I'm the man who'll tell you twice.}
{205}{}{Ask away. I've got one eye and two ears, and the ears are bored.}
{206}{}{Tell me something else.}
{207}{}{Billy presses two bottles of beer into your hands.}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{What makes this the best town?}
{211}{}{What happened to your eye?}
{212}{}{Is the girl outside yours?}
{213}{}{You must have seen a lot of the wasteland.}
{214}{}{You got one eye!}
{215}{}{Take care, Billy.}
{216}{}{Bye, one-eye man.}

# The town.
{220}{}{Walls a deathclaw would give up on. Clean water, if you don't count the puddle. A sheriff who can't be bought and a saloon keeper who can, so there's balance. And the bomb keeps the rent down.}
{221}{}{Any work here for a stranger?}
{222}{}{Plenty. Walter at the water plant has pipes that leak faster than he can swear at them. There's money put by for whoever makes that bomb safe; ask the sheriff. And Moriarty sells answers.}
{223}{}{You've done most of it yourself. The pipes hold, the bomb's been put to bed, and you've had Moriarty's answers out of him. All that's left in Megaton is drinking, and that pays badly.}
{224}{}{Walls a deathclaw would give up on. Clean water. A saloon keeper you can buy and a bomb you can lean on, thanks to you. Find me another town that has its own monument and didn't have to carve it.}
{225}{}{The big one's the bomb. There's good money put by for whoever makes it safe, and nobody here knows which end bites. Ask at the sheriff's house. After that, try the water plant or the saloon.}
{226}{}{Walter, at the water plant. His pipes leak faster than he can swear at them, and he pays cash to anyone who'll hold a wrench. Not much else going.}
{227}{}{Not work, exactly. But Moriarty at the saloon sells answers, if you've got questions. Bring money. Bring more than you think.}

# The eye.
{230}{}{Radscorpion, outside Klamath. Big as a wagon. I got the stinger and it got the eye. Fair trade.}
{231}{}{Card game in the Den. Fella said I was looking at his hand. He was right, so he fixed it.}
{232}{}{Honestly? I walked into a door. But I walked into it guarding a caravan, so it counts.}
{233}{}{That isn't what you told me before.}
{234}{}{It's my eye. I'll lose it any way I like.}

# Maggie.
{240}{}{Maggie? She is now. Found her under a wagon after raiders hit her folks' caravan. She wouldn't come out for the guards; she came out for a strip of jerky. I only had the one, so I retired. Somebody had to watch her eat it.}

# The roads.
{250}{}{All of it worth seeing, and most that isn't. Klamath smells of gecko and the Den smells of worse. Out there a wall is the difference between a town and a camp site. Remember that when somebody calls this place a hole.}

# Low Intelligence.
{260}{}{That I do! And you've got two, and between us that makes three, which is more than most town meetings can count to. You're all right.}
